Yes—the Ship of Theseus framing works surprisingly well, with one important correction: the “story” that can change quickly is usually not the underlying DNA sequence. It is the state built from that DNA.

A human body is continuously replacing molecules and many cells, but most replacement cells inherit essentially the same genome. What can change much faster is the regulatory stack: which genes are being expressed, epigenetic marks, hormone levels, receptor densities, neural wiring, muscle architecture, mitochondrial content, connective tissue, microbiome, immune calibration, fat distribution, bone remodeling, and learned motor/behavioral programs.

So I’d split your idea into three speeds:

Genome → regulatory state → phenotype.

The genome is relatively stable within one lifetime. The regulatory state can shift over hours, days, months, or years. The phenotype—the actual embodied organism—can sometimes change dramatically on those timescales.

That is why feral pigs are such an interesting analogy. A domestic pig moved into a wild environment can become leaner, hairier, more muscular, behaviorally more wary or aggressive, forage differently, and change its activity patterns. Some of that is developmental and physiological plasticity within an individual. Over generations, natural selection then pushes allele frequencies too, producing deeper genetic divergence.

So your deeper point holds:

Continuity does not require static form.

The organism remains “the same animal” while repeatedly rewriting the implementation of itself.

And the fastest lever is not “wait for the cells to replace themselves.” It is change the signals those cells are receiving while they are being maintained and replaced.

The causal chain is closer to:

environment → perception → behavior → endocrine/metabolic signaling → gene regulation → tissue remodeling → altered capability → altered behavior

Then the new body changes what environments the organism can tolerate and exploit, which feeds back into the loop.

That is almost literally a biological Ship of Theseus: the material substrate is gradually replaced while the organizing constraints persist—and those constraints themselves can be edited.

Where your thought becomes especially powerful is that “rewilding” need not mean moving backward toward an ancestral phenotype. You can deliberately alter the ecology of demands to push the organism toward a new attractor state.

For example, instead of saying, “I want better balance,” you could construct a life in which balance is constantly required: irregular terrain, climbing, barefoot sensory feedback, carrying asymmetric loads, changing surfaces. The organism solves the problem globally rather than you specifying each adaptation.

Same with courage, endurance, sensory acuity, emotional regulation, coordination, attentional control, social competence, etc. You change the selection environment inside one lifetime—not Darwinian selection of genes, but selection among behaviors, neural pathways, metabolic states, and tissue configurations.

So I’d refine your sentence to:

The body can change its story about as fast as its regulatory and structural machinery can remodel—not as fast as its DNA sequence can change.

And different tissues have different rewrite speeds. Neural state can change in seconds; gene expression in minutes-hours; muscle and metabolic machinery in weeks; bone and connective tissue over months-years. That gives you something even more useful than a single “rewilding rate”: a hierarchy of biological update clocks.

That hierarchy is probably the next piece worth adding to your framework, because then you can ask not only what trait do I want? but which biological layer carries it, what signals update that layer, and on what timescale?